Alloy: opengl32: reuse the low mapping of a buffer the driver maps again (32-bit games, macOS).

A 32-bit program sees only the low 4 GB, but the Mac OpenGL driver returns buffer maps far above that. For each
glMapBuffer(Range) the wow64 thunk remaps the driver's memory into low memory with mach_vm_remap. Direct3D 8/9 games
on WineD3D map a streaming buffer for nearly every draw, so a frame of 3000 draws made 3000 remaps, and a 32-bit
Direct3D 9 test ran at 36 fps against 106 fps for the same test built 64-bit.

The driver almost always returns the same memory for a buffer. The low mapping from the previous map still shows it
when the host range is still backed by the same VM object in the same VM region: the low mapping holds a reference
to that object, so its ID cannot be reused by another object while we compare. One mach_vm_region query replaces the
remap; any change (other address, region or object, or a larger map) remaps as before.

Measured with Alloy's Direct3D 9 test (3000 draws and 8 blended full-screen layers per frame, 32-bit, M4):
36 fps before, 47 fps after; 26008 of 26011 maps reused the mapping; the frame is pixel-identical.

